<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by tauri.conf.json's
  `bundle.macOS.infoPlist`. Tauri owns every other key in there; this file adds
  only the ones it has no setting for.

  NSCameraUsageDescription is not optional: macOS refuses camera access to any
  app whose Info.plist does not declare a purpose string, so without it the
  Camera scan screen's getUserMedia never yields a stream in the packaged app
  (it works in a browser, which carries the browser's own declaration). There is
  deliberately NO NSMicrophoneUsageDescription — the scanner asks for
  `{ video: …, audio: false }`, so WebKit requests the camera alone, and
  declaring a microphone purpose we never use would be a privacy claim we do not
  need to make.

  When this app is signed with the hardened runtime for notarization it will
  ALSO need the `com.apple.security.device.camera` entitlement; the plist key
  alone covers the current unsigned/local build.

  CFBundleIconName points at the app icon inside Contents/Resources/Assets.car,
  which is how macOS 26 finds the layered icon and gives it the Liquid Glass
  treatment. It is the name of the `.icon` document — Cubus.icon — with the
  extension dropped, and actool bakes that same name into the catalogue via
  --app-icon, so all three must be renamed together. Renaming the document
  without the other two produces a catalogue with no matching asset, and macOS
  falls back to the .icns without a word about it.

  Tauri's own CFBundleIconFile (icons/icon.icns) is left in place and is not
  redundant: macOS 26 prefers the catalogue, and everything older ignores
  CFBundleIconName entirely and falls back to the .icns.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
	<string>Cubus</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Cubus reads your cube's six sides through the camera. Everything happens on this device — no image is stored or sent anywhere.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
